const page = {
  n: '19',
  subtitle: 'Compare Backyard Building Companies by What Can Be Verified',
  description: 'How to evaluate a backyard building company before you decide: an eight-step process, what should be verifiable, red flags, sales pitch vs. verifiable standard, and the questions a serious builder should answer.',
  intro: 'A backyard structure is still a construction project on private property. The company you choose affects site planning, materials, workmanship, communication, schedule, payment risk, warranty support and who is accountable when questions come up. A strong builder makes the project easier to understand, not harder.',
  question: 'How Should You Evaluate a Backyard Building Company?',
  answer: [
    'By what it can explain, document and stand behind before construction begins: construction experience, written scope, material standards, site responsibilities, insurance, relevant completed work, warranty terms, communication, payment expectations, the change-order process, and who will actually build and supervise on your property.',
    'A trustworthy builder makes clear what is included, what is optional, what belongs to the homeowner, and what could change the price or schedule. The lowest price is not the lowest risk when the scope is vague.',
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      type: 'steps', id: 'process', eyebrow: 'A Practical Process', title: 'Eight Steps to Evaluate the Builder Before You Commit', nav: 'How to Evaluate',
      items: [
        { title: 'Verify Construction Experience', text: 'The builder should explain site conditions, foundations, framing, materials, drainage, roofing and long-term performance in plain terms, not just broad quality claims.' },
        { title: 'Review the Written Scope', text: 'Size, style, materials, doors, windows, roof, siding, floor, options, inclusions, exclusions, site assumptions and owner responsibilities.' },
